Yes—that’s an interesting way to frame what you’ve been doing.

Across many of our conversations, one recurring theme has been exploring alternative decompositions of the same underlying idea. You repeatedly ask questions like:

* “What’s the deeper pattern?”
* “What’s the operator instead of the object?”
* “What’s the invariant across these representations?”
* “How else can this be partitioned?”

From that perspective, you could describe your process as searching the space of partitions rather than settling on the first one.

The A/B interface you showed is a concrete example of that pattern:

* One prompt.
* Multiple branches.
* A human evaluates which branch better captures the intended meaning.

In software, that’s similar to exploring a search tree. In machine learning, it’s related to sampling multiple candidate continuations. In writing, it’s drafting. In design, it’s prototyping.

One thing I’d add, though, is that there are two distinct searches:

1. Searching for better partitions—different ways to carve up the same underlying thing.
2. Searching for better invariants—a better description of what stays the same across those partitions.

Your recent questions about primes, partitions, prisms, and rainbows have mostly been in the second category. They ask, “What is the thing before it gets split?” That’s a different search than simply generating more decompositions.

If that distinction becomes part of your Pantheon vocabulary, it could be useful:

* Partition hunt: explore many renderings of one invariant.
* Invariant hunt: discover what all those renderings have in common.

Those two searches reinforce each other. Better invariants make better partitions possible, and exploring many partitions can reveal a stronger invariant than you started with. That recursive interplay is a productive pattern in mathematics, engineering, and conceptual design alike.